Package: oursync
Architecture: all
# coreutils, sed and findutils are deliberately absent: they are Essential packages, always
# present, and lintian makes depending on one without a version an error. rclone's floor is the
# measured one - tools/rclone-floor-test.sh passes on 1.60.1, which is what Ubuntu 24.04 and
# Mint 22 ship. fuse3 is what provides fusermount3.
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         rclone (>= 1.60),
         fuse3
Recommends: nemo, libnotify-bin
Description: Google Drive as a filesystem, with chosen files kept locally
 OurSync presents Google Drive as an ordinary directory tree, by default at
 ~/Drive. Nothing is downloaded until something reads it, so a Drive far larger
 than the local disk costs nothing to mount, and what has been read is cached
 and evicted as the cache fills. A file that must always be fast, or must
 survive going offline, is pinned: a real copy is placed on local disk, served
 from there, and never evicted.
 .
 Pinning is what OurSync adds to rclone, whose cache is eviction-based with no
 way to keep a chosen file. A union remote puts a local pin directory in front
 of the Drive remote, so a pinned file is served from disk deterministically,
 and a write to it reaches both copies.
 .
 The mount is read-write when the Drive remote allows it. Changes made through
 the mount reach Drive by themselves; changes made to Drive elsewhere reach a
 streamed file within the poll interval and a pinned file when the refresh
 timer runs.
 .
 Right-click entries for Nemo are included. Each user runs "oursync setup"
 once, which builds the per-user configuration and walks the one-time Google
 Cloud step: OurSync ships no OAuth client ID, because an application
 distributing its own client ID with Google's restricted drive scope needs
 OAuth verification and an annual third-party security assessment.
